      "id": "q16",
      "question": "Am I the only one who loves these videos but in the end still has no idea which to buy? Please just tell me: buy this one, or these 3. I can't decide between 25 options. [t-shirts]",
      "type": "taste",
      "results": {
        "hybrid": {
          "coverage": "full",
          "answer": "**Not just you. He admits he can't name one \"best\" tee either.** In his newest roundup (Sept 2025) he says almost anyone can make a really good t-shirt now [1], so the best one is whichever matches your preferences [2]. That said, here's the shortest list his own picks support:\n\n**If you buy just one: Uniqlo (~$20).** He calls it \"your starter t-shirt\" [3] and probably the best shirt you can get for the price [4]. If you're taller or wider, get the Muji instead, which is longer and wider [3].\n\n**If you buy three, his value winners:**\n1. **Uniqlo** (or Muji), as above.\n2. **Front General Store** (~$40, made in Japan), if you like a shirt with character and texture [5].\n3. **House of Blanks** (~$35, made in Canada), if you want perfectly smooth with no twisting [6]. He says he leans Front General Store some days and House of Blanks on others [7].\n\nOne caveat: he forgot Bronson in that video and noted it usually wins or comes close [8]. In June 2025 he said Bronson's standard loopwheel tubular tee would probably take the value win [9].\n\n**If you want to spend more, his personal favourite in each category (Sept 2025):**\n- **Everyday mid-range:** J.Press x American Trench, for its rougher hand feel [10]. The 3Sixteen two-pack (~$49) is his pick for best mid-range deal [11].\n- **Japanese:** Wonder Looper, for a more universal fit [12]. He opens that section with a disclosure of his close ties to the brand [13].\n- **Heavyweight:** American Giant Everest, \"by a long shot\" [12].\n- **Clean and minimal:** Merz b. Schwanen 2M15 edges out Lady White for him [14].",
