        "full": {
          "coverage": "full",
          "answer": "**You're not alone. He says himself there's no single perfect tee.** In his biggest roundup (Sept 2025) he notes that \"99.999% of people\" can make a good t-shirt now, so the best one is whichever matches your preferences [1]. That roundup still ends with his personal winners, so here is a short list built from them.\n\n**If you want just 3:**\n1. **Cheap starter: Uniqlo.** At $20 he calls it probably the best t-shirt you can get for the price [2]. If you'd rather spend a bit more for character, his budget-category pick that day was **Front General Store**, a $40 shirt made in Japan [3][4].\n2. **Everyday all-rounder: J.Press x American Trench.** This was his category winner because he likes its rougher hand feel [5].\n3. **Splurge: Merz b. Schwanen 2M15.** It \"eekes out\" Lady White in his favourite category, the clean minimalist tees [6].\n\n**Swap one in if this describes you:**\n- **You want it perfectly smooth with no twisting:** House of Blanks, or 3Sixteen if you want the mid-range option [7][8].\n- **You want a heavyweight:** American Giant, his heavyweight winner \"by a long shot\" [9].\n- **You want Japanese:** Wonder Looper was his pick there [10]. He discloses he's good friends with the founder [11].\n- **You're taller or bigger:** Muji, which is significantly longer and wider than Uniqlo [12].\n\nTwo footnotes:\n- He admits he forgot to include Bronson, which he says usually wins or comes close in these comparisons [13].\n- In his 2024 ranking, his overall winner was Asket [14]. In 2025 he sorted shirts into categories instead of crowning one.",
